course: dictionary
lesson: 8230
title: Revise: study again or change
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To revise means to go over what you have learned again, to get ready for a test. «Revising a little on several days helps you remember for longer than revising only the night before.» The clue is the word remember: going back over learning helps it stay in your head.
explain: Revise can also mean to change and improve something you have written or planned. «Good readers revise their predictions as they find new clues.» Here, a reader changes an idea because new clues show it might be wrong. Ask yourself: what do I need to change, and why?
explain: Notice the difference between revising and cramming. Cramming means trying to learn everything at once, the night before. Revising in small amounts over several days works better, because spaced-out practice helps the brain keep what it has learned.
fact: Revise is a verb: revise, revises, revised, revising. The noun is revision.
fact: In the UK, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand, to revise for a test means to study again what you have learned. In the USA people often say «study» or «review».
fact: In writing, revising means improving the ideas and words. The writing steps are draft, revise, edit and publish.
fact: Revision notes are notes you make to help you go over what you have learned.
fact: Scientists revise their ideas when new evidence comes along.
exercise: «Revising a little on several days helps you remember for longer.» What does revising mean here? | Writing a poem about the weather | Losing your place in the book | *Going over what you learned again | Throwing your work in the bin
exercise: Ella says her writer must revise the story. What does she mean? | *Change and improve it | Read it aloud once and stop | Count the number of words | Put it in a drawer and forget it forever
exercise: Which is the better way to prepare for a test? | Not looking at your notes at all | Cramming everything the night before | Waiting until the test to see what it is about | *Revising a little on several days
test: «Scientists revise ideas when new evidence comes.» What does revise mean? | Forget the idea completely | *Change an idea because of new information | Copy the idea into a book | Make up a brand-new word
test: Which word is the noun form of revise? | *Revision | Revised | Revises | Revising
exam: Which statement about revise is true? | Revise only means to change a piece of writing | Revise means to forget something | *Revise can mean to study again to get ready for a test, or to change and improve something | Revise is a noun meaning a test
